{
  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Minnesota Twins were a team that never quite found their footing, spending the entire season treading water below .500 and finishing a disappointing 73-89. Built around a roster that still featured exciting pieces like Byron Buxton, Jorge Polanco, and Miguel Sanó, the Twins entered the year with postseason hopes but were undermined from the very first week by a bullpen — particularly closer Alex Colomé — that simply could not hold leads when it mattered most.\n\nApril was a disaster, with the Twins going 9-15 as Colomé surrendered critical runs in multiple high-leverage appearances, including a brutal three-run error game on April 21 that exemplified the chaos of those early weeks. May offered a glimmer of hope: Sanó caught fire with a string of clutch home runs, Polanco contributed timely singles, and the team clawed to a 13-16 record — not good, but a sign of life. June and the first half of July brought more of the same inconsistency, with Josh Donaldson providing occasional punch and the bullpen continuing to crater in close games.\n\nThe summer months told a tale of two halves within the second half. August was the Twins' best stretch of the season at 14-13, powered by Jorge Polanco's monster three-run homer on August 3rd and a collection of clutch contributions from Mitch Garver and Willians Astudillo. For a moment it felt like the Twins might make a run, but the wins never strung together long enough to matter in the standings. September brought the curtain down quietly — Taylor Rogers struggled in the closer role, Colomé allowed Aaron Judge to crush a three-run homer in a pivotal September game, and the Twins finished with a whimper at 13-14 in September and 2-1 in October, their only winning month outside of August coming too late to salvage a lost season.",
  "chapters": [
